---
import CaseContents from "./CaseContents.astro";
---
<article class="ck-case">
  <header><div class="meta case-meta"><span class="case-meta__client-full">Carrabba’s Italian Grill / Bloomin’ Brands</span><span class="case-meta__client-short">Carrabba’s</span><span class="case-meta__highlight">40% faster access</span></div><div class="intro"><div><p>Content strategy &amp; UX architecture</p><h1>From table<br />to kitchen.</h1></div><div><p class="lede">Carrabba's Tableside aimed to reach food enthusiasts beyond existing restaurant guests through recipes, techniques, and chef expertise.</p><dl><div><dt>Role</dt><dd>Senior UX Strategist</dd></div><div><dt>Result</dt><dd>40% faster time-to-recipe</dd></div></dl></div></div></header>
  <CaseContents links={[{"href": "#case-section-1", "label": "The brief"}, {"href": "#case-section-2", "label": "Content architecture"}, {"href": "#case-section-3", "label": "Reading experience"}, {"href": "#case-section-4", "label": "Brand details"}, {"href": "#case-section-5", "label": "Outcome"}]} />
  <figure class="hero"><img src="/images/carrabbas_hero.webp" alt="Carrabba’s restaurant kitchen" loading="eager" /></figure>
<section class="ck-detail" aria-labelledby="ck-brief"><div><p class="ck-detail__label">The brief</p><h2 id="ck-brief">Make the content worth coming back for.</h2></div><div class="ck-detail__copy"><p>As Senior UX Strategist through Voce Communications and Porter Novelli, I led UX, information architecture, visual design, and the WordPress build, using Secrets from Carrabba's Kitchen as the editorial anchor.</p></div></section>
  <section class="opening" id="case-section-1" tabindex="-1"><div><p class="eyebrow">01 / Reframing the job</p><h2>Teach the technique.<br />Earn the return visit.</h2></div><div><p>Moderated sessions with twelve home cooks across three skill levels revealed different discovery habits: experienced cooks sought techniques and ingredients; novices sought dishes or achievable outcomes.</p><p>Audits of eight food platforms and three restaurant blogs reinforced the distinction between finding inspiration and following instructions.</p></div></section>
  <section class="blueprint" id="case-section-2" tabindex="-1"><figure><img src="/images/carrabbas01.webp" alt="Carrabba’s Kitchen wireframes" loading="lazy" /></figure><div><p class="eyebrow">02 / Content architecture</p><h2>Many ways in.<br />One clear way through.</h2><p>Technique and ingredient categories, Popular, Recent, and topical filters offered routes into the Kitchen Secrets library without requiring an exact recipe name.</p><p>Informative previews and skill-based titles such as “How to Boil the Perfect Pot of Pasta” helped readers judge relevance before opening an article.</p><figure><img src="/images/carrabbas02.webp" alt="Carrabba’s Kitchen mobile recipe discovery" loading="lazy" /></figure></div></section>
  <figure class="mobile-field"><img src="/images/carrabbas03.webp" alt="Carrabba’s Kitchen across mobile screens" loading="lazy" /></figure>
  <section class="reading" id="case-section-3" tabindex="-1"><div><p class="eyebrow">03 / Made for the kitchen</p><h2>Read, cook,<br />look back.</h2><p>Recipe pages reduced competing elements through vertical layouts, large targets, and clear ingredients and instructions, designed for occupied hands, distant screens, and interrupted attention.</p></div><div class="reading__images"><figure><img src="/images/carrabbas04.webp" alt="Carrabba’s Kitchen mobile interface collection" loading="lazy" /></figure><figure><img src="/images/carrabbas05.webp" alt="Carrabba’s Kitchen chef portrait" loading="lazy" /></figure><figure><img src="/images/carrabbas06.webp" alt="Carrabba’s pasta recipe" loading="lazy" /></figure></div></section>
  <section class="warmth" id="case-section-4" tabindex="-1"><figure><img src="/images/carrabbas07.webp" alt="Carrabba’s family food moment" loading="lazy" /></figure><div><p class="eyebrow">04 / Brand in the details</p><h2>Warmth without clutter.</h2><p>In-process food photography explained techniques; Tableside serif titles and sans-serif body copy paired brand recognition with mobile readability.</p><p>I coordinated the visual language with the main website's agency so the sites felt related while serving different tasks.</p></div></section>
<section class="ck-detail" aria-labelledby="ck-editorial"><div><p class="ck-detail__label">Editorial system &amp; handoff</p><h2 id="ck-editorial">Give the content team the reasoning behind the layouts.</h2></div><div class="ck-detail__copy"><p>Editorial guidelines covered action-oriented titles, content length, images, and tagging to preserve the architecture as editors added material.</p></div></section>
  <section class="outcome" id="case-section-5" tabindex="-1"><div><p class="eyebrow">05 / Outcome</p><h2>A system the<br />content team could keep cooking with.</h2><div class="stats"><div><b>40%</b><span>faster time-to-recipe</span></div><div><b>28%</b><span>more mobile session depth</span></div><div><b>3 weeks</b><span>to independent publishing</span></div></div><p class="outcome__context">The original project account reports 40% faster content access in task-timed sessions with twelve participants, 28% greater mobile session depth after launch, and independent publishing within three weeks. These are distinct measures, not evidence that one design change caused the engagement increase.</p></div><figure><img src="/images/carrabbas08.webp" alt="Carrabba’s Kitchen responsive publishing system" loading="lazy" /></figure></section>
<section class="ck-detail" aria-labelledby="ck-reflection"><div><p class="ck-detail__label">Reflection</p><h2 id="ck-reflection">Observe the experience in a working kitchen.</h2></div><div class="ck-detail__copy"><p>I would observe people cooking with the platform to understand where they lose their place, revisit ingredients, or stop following a recipe.</p><p>The existing sessions tested navigation; contextual observation would explain what analytics alone could only flag for further research.</p></div></section>
</article>
<style>
